The open int8 conv's 11.3x gap to the vendor is ISSUE RATE, and it is visible statically
2026-09-09. Desktop 2 (Phoenix, Ryzen 7 8700G), native Windows, mlir-aie ironenv (v1.4.2,
llvm-aie 22.0.0.2026090201). One short NPU run to rebuild the design; the analysis itself
reads object code and takes no context. `xrt-smi examine -r aie-partitions` reported no
hardware contexts before the build.

Commands (from the repo root, in the ironenv):
  python kernels/bottleneck_sweep/sweep.py --shapes 32x32 --iters 10 --warmup 3
  python tools/aie_disasm.py ~/.npu/cache/<conv>/conv2dk1_i8_*.o --loops --regs --verbose
  python tools/aie_disasm.py ~/.npu/cache/<conv>/conv2dk3_ui8_*.o --loops --regs --verbose
  python tools/aie_bank_check.py ~/.npu/cache/<conv> --kernel <conv2dk3 symbol>

WHY. docs/SILICON.md 2.3 puts the vendor DPU at 1650 GOPS per column on int8 conv against the
open ml/bottleneck kernel's 146.1 GOPS -- 4.5% of what the same column does under AMD's own
compiler, an 11.3x gap that is clock-independent because both ran on the same silicon.
Objective K1 says of it: "the first trace will say whether it is data movement or issue rate."
No trace was ever run, and docs/BENCHMARKS.md records why: "The conv kernels have no surviving
build cache, so this repo's most-lost op class was not surveyed." The cache is rebuilt here and
the question is answered without a trace at all, from the instruction schedule.

A NOTE ON WHAT WAS ALREADY CLOSED, BECAUSE IT IS EASY TO MISREAD. kernels/README.md closed a
"kernel quality" item with "both vectorize correctly with aie::mmul". That is a CORRECTNESS
statement and it is true. It is not a throughput statement, and the numbers below are the
throughput one.

