--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. READING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
IT IS ISSUE RATE, DECISIVELY, AND K1's QUESTION IS ANSWERED WITHOUT A TRACE. The int8 GEMM
issues 0.889 vmac per cycle. The best conv loop in the whole design issues 0.333, the 3x3's
main loop issues 0.222, and the 1x1's hot loop issues 0.045 -- with two of its three loops
issuing no MAC at all. A 4x to 20x shortfall in MAC issue density, against a measured 11.3x
throughput gap. The schedule alone more than accounts for the gap; nothing about data movement
needs to be invoked to explain it.

THE TWO KERNELS FAIL FOR DIFFERENT REASONS, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS SUBTLE.

The 1x1 (section 3) never keeps an accumulator in a register. Its 22-bundle loop loads all
four quarters of the accumulator from memory (vlda amhh1/amhl1/amlh1/amll1), issues ONE vmac,
and stores four quarters back (vst amhh2/amhl2/amlh2/amll2). Six of the 22 bundles are
entirely nop -- exposed pipeline latency waiting on the accumulator load. The kernel names
three accumulator registers, cm0 to cm2, out of a file of nine. This is not a subtle
scheduling loss: the accumulator is memory-resident when the hardware has room to keep it
resident, and the fix is the one mm.cc already demonstrates.

The 3x3 (section 4) does keep its accumulators live -- cm1 through cm4, no accumulator
traffic to memory in the loop -- and still reaches only 0.222 vmac per cycle, because six of
its eighteen bundles are vshift and four more are vmov. Those are the sliding-window
realignment for a 3x3 convolution, done in the core's issue slots. This is the classic
conv-on-SIMD cost, and it is exactly what K1's own tooling section proposes removing:
"im2col or row shifting done by the mem tile's 4-D BDs rather than by core code". The static
read confirms the proposal and prices it: about half the issue slots of the best conv loop
are window alignment rather than arithmetic.

A MINOR THIRD FINDING. tools/aie_bank_check.py reports one paired-load bundle inside the 3x3's
hot loop and a bank holding two distinct buffers, so the same-bank penalty measured elsewhere
(results/aie/bank_conflict_survey.log) applies here too. At one bundle in eighteen it is worth
about 5%, which is noise beside a 4x issue-density shortfall. Recorded so it is not mistaken
for a lever.

WHAT IT MEANS FOR K1. K1's go/no-go is "nothing below ~1 TOPS per column is worth a second
week". Reaching 1 TOPS from 146 GOPS needs 6.8x. The 1x1's defect is worth up to 20x on that
kernel alone and is a known-good pattern away; the 3x3's needs the DMA-side rework K1 already
specifies. So the gap is NOT evidence that the silicon cannot do it -- the same column does
1650 GOPS under AMD's compiler -- and it is not evidence that the op class is intrinsically
lost. It is evidence that these two kernels leave most of their issue slots empty. Whether
closing that is worth the week is a judgement this log does not make; what it removes is the
excuse that nobody knew where the 11.3x went.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS DOES NOT SHOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Issue density per loop is not weighted by trip count. Which loop dominates the runtime was
  not measured, so "the schedule more than accounts for the gap" rests on every candidate loop
  being far below the GEMM, not on a weighted average. A trace or a trip-count analysis would
  sharpen it.
- The rebuild ran one shape (32x32) for one sitting at 10 iterations, and its 95.5 hw GOPS is
  a single point that includes fixed cost, not the 146.1 GOPS marginal fit it is compared
  against. The 146.1 and 1650 figures are quoted from earlier logs, not re-measured here.
- No claim is made that a rewritten kernel WOULD reach 1 TOPS. The 20x and 4x are ceilings on
  what the schedule leaves on the table, not predictions of a rewrite.
- One column, one dtype, one bottleneck configuration, one optimisation level.
- The vendor's 1650 GOPS is measured on yolov8l through the DPU, a different graph and
  different layer shapes from this bottleneck. It is the bar this repo chose; it is not a
  like-for-like kernel comparison.
